L000031	\begin{document}
L000032	
L000033	\author{Andreas Dechant}
L000034	\affiliation{Department of Physics \#1, Graduate School of Science, Kyoto University, Kyoto 606-8502, Japan}
L000035	\title{Finite-frequency fluctuation-response inequality}
L000036	\date{\today}
L000037	
L000038	\begin{abstract}
L000039	We derive an inequality relating the finite-frequency linear response and fluctuations of an observable in a physical system.
L000040	The relation holds for arbitrary observables and perturbations in general Markovian dynamics, including over- and underdamped Langevin systems and jump processes, both in and out of equilibrium.
L000041	As a consequence, we obtain a universal upper bound on the broad-band signal-to-noise ratio of noisy dynamics, which only depends on the damping constant and temperature.
L000042	We further show that the inequality reduces to an equality for appropriately chosen observables or perturbations in linear systems, both overdamped and underdamped and both in and out of equilibrium.
L000043	\end{abstract}
L000044	
L000045	\maketitle
L000046	
L000047	Fluctuations and response are equally important for measurements of small noisy systems:
L000048	On the one hand, when probing a system by measuring its response to an external perturbation, the accuracy of the measurement is limited by the fluctuations of the system due to environmental noise.
L000049	When the system acts as a sensor, the ratio of response and fluctuations corresponds to the signal-to-noise ratio.
L000050	On the other hand, a measurement of the fluctuations themselves can also reveal information about the dynamics of the system.
L000051	This connection is most prominent in equilibrium systems, where response and fluctuations are directly proportional and related by the fluctuation-dissipation theorem \cite{Kubo1966,Kubo2012}.
L000052	
L000053	Out of equilibrium, this intimate connection between response and fluctuations is lost.
L000054	A phenomenological fluctuation-dissipation theorem may be formulated by introducing an effective temperature \cite{Cugliandolo1997,Loi2008,Cugliandolo2011,Szamel2014,Lippiello2014}, however, the latter depends on the type of measurement \cite{Fielding2002}.
L000055	A non-equilibrium fluctuation-dissipation theorem may also be derived from the underlying dynamics \cite{Agarwal1972,Haenggi1982a,Marconi2008,Baiesi2009,Seifert2010a}, expressing the response as fluctuations of the unperturbed system.
L000056	However, these fluctuations are expressed using formal conjugate observables that are not directly measurable, thus limiting practical applications.
L000057	
L000058	More recent results establish relations between response and fluctuations even for out-of-equilibrium systems \cite{Dechant2020,Owen2020,FernandesMartins2023,Gao2024,Aslyamov2024,Ptaszynski2024,Liu2025,VanVu2025}, whose common feature is that they are formulated in the form of inequalities rather than equalities like the fluctuation-dissipation theorem.
L000059	However, from the point of view of applications, these results suffer from two drawbacks:
L000060	First, most results are formulated for discrete systems \cite{Owen2020,FernandesMartins2023,Aslyamov2024,Ptaszynski2024,VanVu2025} and not straightforward to extend to continuous systems.
L000061	By contrast, experiments are frequently performed in continuous systems, be it active matter \cite{Loi2011a,Fodor2016}, granular materials \cite{Song2005}, biological systems \cite{Sato2003,Chen2007,Mizuno2008} or levitated nano-particles \cite{Hempston2017,GonzalezBallestero2021}.
L000062	
L000063	Second, and more seriously, existing results only apply to the static response of a system, where the perturbation is constant in time.
L000064	Conversely, in experiments, it is more common to work with time-periodic perturbations and to characterize response and fluctuations in frequency space \cite{Lau2003,Brau2007,Wilhelm2008,BenIsaac2011,Loi2011a}.
L000065	This has practical reasons, as observing the static response can be challenging in small systems---a tracer particle inside a biological cell subjected to a constant force will eventually collide with the cell wall.
L000066	But measuring response at different frequencies also yields additional information about the dynamics and structure of the system that is not contained in the static, zero-frequency response.
L000067	
L000068	In this work, we derive a fluctuation-response inequality (FRI) relating the finite-frequency response and fluctuations of general observables, applicable to both discrete and continuous systems.
L000069	The ratio between the magnitude of the linear response function and the power spectral density of an arbitrary observable at any frequency is upper bounded by a frequency-independent constant, depending on the temperature and friction coefficient of the environment.
L000070	As a corollary, we obtain a universal upper bound on the frequency-integrated signal-to-noise ratio of noisy sensors.
L000071	Moreover, we show that equality in the FRI is attained for linear dynamics, both in and out of equilibrium, which indicates
L000072	
L000073	
L000074	
L000075	\textit{Setup.}
L000076	In this work, we focus on Langevin dynamics \cite{Risken1986,Coffey2017} describing the noisy time-evolution of a set of $D$ continuous degrees of freedom $\bm{x}(t) = (x_1(t),\ldots,x_D(t))$.
L000077	The dynamics can be overdamped, in which case
L000078	\begin{align}
L000079	\dot{\bm{x}}(t) = \frac{1}{\gamma} \big(\bm{f}(\bm{x}(t)) + \epsilon \bm{h}(\bm{x}(t),t) \big) + \sqrt{\frac{2 \bm{T}}{\gamma}} \bm{\xi}(t) \label{langevin-over},
L000080	\end{align}
L000081	or underdamped, in which case
L000082	\begin{align}
L000083	m \dot{\bm{v}}(t) = - \gamma \bm{v}(t) + \bm{f}(\bm{x}(t)) + \epsilon \bm{h}(\bm{x}(t),t) + \sqrt{2 \gamma \bm{T}} \bm{\xi}(t) \label{langevin-under}.
L000084	\end{align}
L000085	Here, $\bm{v}(t) = \dot{\bm{x}}(t)$ is the velocity, $\bm{f}(\bm{x})$ are coordinate-dependent forces (interactions or conservative and non-conservative external forces), and $\bm{\xi}(t)$ is a vector of uncorrelated white Gaussian noises.
L000086	All degrees of freedom have the same mass $m$ and friction coefficient $\gamma$ and are in contact with equilibrium environments, whose temperatures are the entries of the diagonal matrix $\bm{T}$.
L000087	More general situations, including non-thermal environments, velocity-dependent forces and jump processes on a discrete state space are discussed in Appendix A.
L000088	$\bm{h}(\bm{x},t)$ is a perturbation force; we focus on the linear response regime $\epsilon \ll 1$.
L000089	We assume that, for $\epsilon = 0$, the dynamics described by \eqref{langevin-over} or \eqref{langevin-under} is in a steady state with probability density $p_\text{st}(\bm{x})$ or $p_\text{st}(\bm{x},\bm{v})$.
L000090	
L000091	We measure a set of $K$ observables $z_k(t) = z_k(\bm{x}(t))$, $k = 1,\ldots,K$, which are functions of the coordinates of the system.
L000092	We quantify the fluctuations of the observables using the spectral-density matrix $\bmc{S}(\omega)$, defined as the Fourier transform of the steady-state covariance \cite{Stratonovich1963}
L000093	\begin{align}
L000094	\mathcal{S}_{kl}(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty dt \ e^{i \omega t} \text{Cov}(z_k(t),z_l(0)) \label{psd} .
L000095	\end{align}
L000096	
L000097	We further consider a set of $Q$ time-dependent perturbations to \eqref{langevin-over} or \eqref{langevin-under},
L000098	\begin{align}
L000099	\bm{h}(\bm{x},t) = \sum_{q=1}^Q \bm{g}_q(\bm{x}) \phi_q(t) \label{langevin-perturbed} .
L000100	\end{align}
L000101	Here, $\bm{g}_q(\bm{x})$ are coordinate-dependent forces and $\phi_q(t)$ are time-dependent functions.
L000102	In the limit $\epsilon \rightarrow 0$, the response of the observables to the perturbations can to leading order be expressed using the linear-response matrix $\bm{R}(t)$ \cite{Haenggi1982a},
L000103	\begin{align}
L000104	\lim_{\epsilon \rightarrow 0} \frac{\Av{z_k}_{t,\epsilon} - \Av{z_k}_\text{st}}{\epsilon} = \sum_{q = 1}^{Q} \int_0^t dt' \ R_{k q}(t-t') \phi_q(t') \label{response} ,
L000105	\end{align}
L000106	where we assume that the system is initially in the steady state with average $\av{z_k}_\text{st}$.
L000107	$\Av{z_k}_{t,\epsilon}$ denotes the average value of $z_k(t)$ when applying the perturbation.
L000108	$R_{kq}(t-t')$ expresses the impact of the perturbation $q$ at time $t'$ on the average of the observable $k$ at the later time $t$.
L000109	Equivalently, we have in Fourier space,
L000110	\begin{align}
L000111	\bmc{R}(\omega) = \int_0^\infty dt \ e^{i \omega t} \bm{R}(t),
L000112	\end{align}
L000113	where we used that $\bm{R}(t) = 0$ for $t < 0$.
L000114	
L000115	\textit{Fluctuation-response inequality.}
L000116	\begin{figure}
L000117	\includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{perpot-fffri.pdf}
L000118	\caption{Power spectral density (dashed lines) and magnitude of the response (solid lines) of the velocity of an underdampled particle in a periodic potential, \eqref{langevin-perpot}.
L000119	The black lines are for vanishing tilt $f_0 = 0$, where the system is in equilibrium.
L000120	The orange lines are for a non-equilibrium steady state with $f_0 = 0.175 \cdot 2 \pi$.
L000121	Other parameter values are $m = 1, \gamma = 1/3, T = 1/2, U_0 = 1$ and $L = 1$.
L000122	Both in and out of equilibrium, the power spectral density upper bounds the response, as predicted by \eqref{fffri-1D}.}
L000123	\label{fig-perpot}
L000124	\end{figure}
L000125	The first main result is the inequality between the response- and spectral-density matrices,
L000126	\begin{gather}
L000127	\bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) \bmc{S}^{-1}(\omega) \bmc{R}(\omega) \leq \bmc{A}  \label{fffri}, \\
L000128	\text{with} \qquad \mathcal{A}_{qr} = \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \Av{ \bm{g}_q \cdot \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{g}_r}_\text{st} \n .
L000129	\end{gather}
L000130	where H denotes the Hermitian conjugate.
L000131	The matrix inequality \eqref{fffri} means that the difference of the right-hand and left-hand side is positive semi-definite.
L000132	Since the right-hand side is independent of frequency and the choice of the observables, this relation yields a strong constraint between the response and fluctuations of observables in Markovian systems, irrespective of whether the system is in or out of equilibrium.
L000133	This generalizes the linear fluctuation-response inequality (FRI) derived in Ref.~\cite{Dechant2020} to more general observables and finite frequencies; we therefore refer to \eqref{fffri} as the finite-frequency FRI.
L000134	For a single perturbation $\bm{g}(\bm{x}) \phi(t)$, the response matrix reduces to the vector of response functions and the matrix $\bmc{A}$ to the scalar $\mathcal{A} = \av{\bm{g}\cdot \bm{T} \bm{g}}_\text{st}/(2\gamma)$.
L000135	In this case, we definite the response efficiency
L000136	\begin{align}
L000137	\eta^R(\omega) = \frac{\bmc{R}^*(\omega) \cdot \bmc{S}^{-1}(\omega) \bmc{R}(\omega)}{\mathcal{A}} \leq 1 \label{response-efficiency},
L000138	\end{align}
L000139	where $*$ denotes complex conjugation.
L000140	The response efficiency satisfies $0 \leq \eta^R(\omega) \leq 1$ and quantifies the magnitude of the observed response in relation to the fluctuations of the observables.
L000141	It allows characterizing the sensitivity to perturbations in a unified manner, enabling comparisons between different systems, observables and perturbations.
L000142	For example, a weakly trapped particle is susceptible to perturbations and exhibits a large response, however, it is also susceptible to fluctuations and its measurement does necessarily not yield more information about the perturbation than a strongly trapped particle with small response and small fluctuations.
L000143	For a single observable and a single perturbation ($K = Q = 1$), \eqref{fffri} and \eqref{response-efficiency} simplify to
L000144	\begin{align}
L000145	\big\vert \mathcal{R}(\omega) \big\vert^2 \leq \mathcal{A} S(\omega), \qquad \eta^R(\omega) = \frac{\vert \mathcal{R}(\omega) \vert^2}{\mathcal{S}(\omega) \mathcal{A}} \label{fffri-1D}.
L000146	\end{align}
L000147	Thus, the response of any observable to a perturbation with frequency $\omega$ is bounded by the fluctuations of the observable at the same frequency times a constant.
L000148	If the functional form of the perturbation is known, then the right hand side can be evaluated from the steady-state statistics of the system.
L000149	Therefore, \eqref{fffri-1D} allows us to infer a bound on the response from a passive measurement of the system.
L000150	
L000151	\textit{Response and fluctuations in and out of equilibrium.}
L000152	To demonstrate this bound, we consider an underdamped particle in a tilted periodic potential
L000153	\begin{align}
L000154	m \dot{v}(t) &= - \gamma v(t) - U'(x(t)) + f_0  + \epsilon g_0 \phi(t) + \sqrt{2 \gamma T} \xi(t) \label{langevin-perpot} ,
L000155	\end{align}
L000156	where $U(x) = U_0 (1-\cos(2 \pi x/L))$.
L000157	The constant force $f_0$ tilts the potential and drives the system into a non-equilibrium steady state with a finite average velocity $\av{v}_\text{st}$ at long times.
L000158	We remark that, despite the apparent simplicity of \eqref{langevin-perpot}, underdamped diffusion in periodic potentials is not completely understood from a theoretical point of view and possesses a rich structure of interacting dynamical effects \cite{Vollmer1983,Lindenberg2005,Marchenko2012,Lindner2016,Fischer2018,Fischer2020,Spiechowicz2023}.
L000159	We focus on the fluctuations and response of the velocity and compute the power spectrum $\mathcal{S}_v(\omega)$ and response $\mathcal{R}_v(\omega)$ from numerical simulations (see Appendix D for details).
L000160	The results are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig-perpot}.
L000161	In equilibrium, the velocity power spectrum (black dashed line) shows a pronounced peak at $\omega \approx 2 \pi$, corresponding to oscillations of a trapped particle.
L000162	Since the particle can escape from the potential by thermal fluctuations, at low frequencies, the velocity spectrum saturates at the long-time diffusion coefficient of the particle,
L000163	\begin{align}
L000164	D_x = \frac{1}{2} \lim_{\tau \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\text{Var}(x)}{\tau} = \frac{1}{2} \lim_{\omega \rightarrow 0} S_v(\omega) .
L000165	\end{align}
L000166	At high frequencies, the velocity spectrum decays as $S_v(\omega) \simeq 2 \gamma T/(m^2 \omega^2)$.
L000167	The response spectrum (black solid line) likewise exhibits a peak near the oscillation frequency of the particle, whose height, in agreement with \eqref{fffri-1D}, is reduced compared to the one in the power spectrum.
L000168	We remark that this effect stems from the non-linearity of the trapping force---for a linear oscillator, \eqref{fffri-1D} turns onto an equality.
L000169	Equality in \eqref{fffri-1D} is observed at high frequencies, where fluctuations and response are dominated by the properties of the environment.
L000170	Driving the system out of equilibrium by tilting the potential decreases the effective barrier height, allowing the particle to more easily escape from a minimum of the potential.
L000171	This reduces the power spectrum at the oscillation frequency, while an additional peak appears at the frequency at which the particle traverses the potential.
L000172	Tilting is known to greatly increase the diffusion coefficient \cite{Reimann2001,Lindner2016}, leading to a significant increase in the low-frequency spectrum.
L000173	Both the reduction in the power spectrum at the oscillation frequency and the increased low-frequency fluctuations are mirrored in the behavior of the response.
L000174	In particular, the value of non-equilibrium power spectrum near the oscillation frequency is lower than the response in equilibrium, and thus \eqref{fffri-1D} predicts a reduction of the non-equilibrium response from a passive measurement of the fluctuations.
L000175	
L000176	\textit{Bound on signal-to-noise ratio.}
L000177	The second main result is a consequence of \eqref{fffri-1D} for a single observable $z(t)$ and an equilibrium environment $\bm{T} = T \bm{I}$.
L000178	In this case, the relation
L000179	\begin{align}
L000180	\frac{1}{\pi} \int_0^\infty d\omega \ \mathcal{S}(\omega) = \text{Var}_\text{st}(z)
L000181	\end{align}
L000182	holds, where $\text{Var}_\text{st}(z)$ denotes the steady-state fluctuations of $z(t)$.
L000183	Integrating \eqref{fffri-1D} over frequency yields
L000184	\begin{align}
L000185	\frac{1}{\pi} \int_0^\infty d\omega \ \big\vert \mathcal{R}(\omega) \big\vert^2 \leq \frac{1}{2 \gamma T} \Av{\Vert \bm{g} \Vert^2}_\text{st} \text{Var}_\text{st}(z) .
L000186	\end{align}
L000187	The ratio between the magnitude of the response and the fluctuations is commonly referred to as the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) \cite{Fraden2010},
L000188	\begin{align}
L000189	\text{SNR}(\omega) = \frac{\big\vert \mathcal{R}(\omega) \big\vert}{\sqrt{\text{Var}_\text{st}(z)}} ,
L000190	\end{align}
L000191	which allows rewriting the above inequality as
L000192	\begin{align}
L000193	\int_0^\infty d\omega \ \big(\text{SNR}(\omega)\big)^2 \leq \frac{\pi \av{\Vert \bm{g} \Vert^2}_\text{st}}{2 \gamma T} \label{snr-bound} .
L000194	\end{align}
L000195	Thus, the frequency-integral of the SNR is bounded by the friction coefficient and temperature of the environment, and the overall magnitude of the perturbation, independent of the precise details of the system.
L000196	The left-hand side of \eqref{snr-bound} quantifies the broad-band SNR of the system over all frequencies.
L000197	As we show below, equality is attained when the system consists of a single particle in a harmonic confinement.
L000198	In that sense, a harmonically trapped particle in equilibrium is the best possible broad-band sensor, achieving the largest possible frequency-integrated SNR.
L000199	In practice, however, one is often more interested in the SNR at specific frequencies, which may be substantially enhanced out of equilibrium \cite{Dechant2024}.
L000200	In such a case, \eqref{snr-bound} states that enhancing the SNR beyond the single particle value in a given frequency range necessarily leads to a suppression of the SNR at other frequencies.
L000201	
L000202	\textit{Conservation of response in linear networks.}
L000203	The third main result is that equality in \eqref{fffri} is attained for linear forces, $\bm{f}(\bm{x}) = - \bm{K}(\bm{x} - \bm{x}_\text{eq})$, where $\bm{K}$ is a matrix and $\bm{x}_\text{eq}$ is the equilibrium position.
L000204	In that case, when the observables are the $D$ positions $\bm{x}(t)$ of the particles and the perturbation is a spatially constant force $\bm{g}(\bm{x}) = \bm{g}_0$, we obtain
L000205	\begin{align}
L000206	\bmc{R}_x^*(\omega) \cdot \bmc{S}_x^{-1}(\omega) \bmc{R}_x(\omega) = \mathcal{A} = \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \bm{g} \cdot \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{g} \label{fffri-linear} .
L000207	\end{align}
L000208	This relation is shown below for overdamped systems and in Appendix B for underdamped systems.
L000209	Importantly, this identity holds irrespective of whether the dynamics is in or out of equilibrium.
L000210	For general perturbations, equality only holds when all degrees of freedom of the system are observed.
L000211	Moreover, since \eqref{fffri-linear} is an inequality for systems with non-linear forces, it implies that, for the a given magnitude of the response, non-linear systems exhibit larger fluctuations.
L000212	
L000213	As an example, we consider a linear network of coupled oscillators, depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig-network}.
L000214	When applying a perturbation to one or several oscillators, its effect spreads across the network and we can measure the corresponding response of each oscillator.
L000215	\eqref{fffri-linear} states that the overall response of all oscillators, weighted by their fluctuations, is a conserved quantity.
L000216	In other words, measuring the response and fluctuations of all oscillators allows recovering the magnitude of an arbitrary perturbation; measuring a subset of oscillators generally only results in a lower bound.
L000217	The portion of the perturbation that can be recovered from the measurement is quantified by the response efficiency \eqref{response-efficiency}.
L000218	Fig.~\ref{fig-network-response} shows the response efficiency of the perturbed oscillator (red) and another oscillator in the network (blue).
L000219	We observe that, in the high-frequency limit, the response efficiency of the perturbed oscillator approaches unity; at high frequencies, response and fluctuations are determined by the local properties of the environment.
L000220	At intermediate frequencies, the perturbation excites collective modes in the network and causes a non-vanishing response of other oscillators.
L000221	Interestingly, the response efficiency can be larger for oscillators other than the perturbed one, indicating that a non-local measurement can yield more information about the perturbation.
L000222	
L000223	Measuring more than one oscillator simultaneously provides additional information about the perturbation.
L000224	Measuring the neighboring (light blue) oscillators in addition to the blue one results in increased response efficiency (light blue line in Fig.~\ref{fig-network-response}).
L000225	Strikingly, when measuring the perturbed (red) oscillator and its neighbors (light red) simultaneously, we find an response efficiency of unity for all frequencies (light red line in Fig.~\ref{fig-network-response}).
L000226	As we prove in Appendix C, this finding is generic: In linear networks, the magnitude of the perturbation can always be inferred from a measurement of the perturbed oscillators and their neighbors.
L000227	
L000228	\begin{figure}
L000229	\includegraphics[width=.35\textwidth]{response-efficiency-net.pdf}
L000230	\caption{A network consisting of 21 oscillators (disks), coupled to each other by springs with spring constant $\kappa = 0.75$; the outermost ring is attached to the black points by springs with spring constant $k = 2$.
L000231	The mass of each oscillator is $m = 1$, the damping constant is $\gamma = 0.01$ and the temperature $T = 1$.
L000232	The perturbation is applied to the red oscillator.}
L000233	\label{fig-network}
L000234	\end{figure}
L000235	
L000236	\begin{figure}
L000237	\includegraphics[width=.49\textwidth]{response-efficiency-only.pdf}
L000238	\caption{Response efficiency of a linear network.
L000239	The response efficiency \eqref{response-efficiency} of the perturbed oscillator (red line) approaches $1$ in the high-frequency limit, where the perturbation is localized.
L000240	At intermediate frequencies, the response efficiency in other parts of the network (blue) can be larger.
L000241	Also measuring the response of the neighbors (light blue) yields additional information about the perturbation and increases the response efficiency.
L000242	In particular, measuring the perturbed oscillator and its neighbors (light red) results in a response efficiency of unity at all frequencies. }
L000243	\label{fig-network-response}
L000244	\end{figure}
L000245	
L000246	\textit{Proof of the FRI for overdamped dynamics.}
L000247	In the interest of conciseness, we focus on the overdamped case \eqref{langevin-over} here; the derivation for more general overdamped dynamics, as well as underdamped Langevin and Markov jump dynamics is provided in SM A.
L000248	
L000249	
L000250	
L000251	
L000252	
L000253	
L000254	We consider a set of $K$ observables of the form
L000255	\begin{align}
L000256	r_k(t) = z_k(\bm{x}(t)) + \bm{w}_k(\bm{x}(t)) \circ \dot{\bm{x}}(t) \label{observable-current} .
L000257	\end{align}
L000258	This is slightly more general than the form discussed above, since it also contains a current-like contribution, where $\circ$ denotes the Stratonovich product.
L000259	We denote the steady-state average of $r_k(t)$ by $\Av{r_k}_\text{st}$.
L000260	Next, the response of the observables is calculated.
L000261	A central result of non-equilibrium linear response theory \cite{Agarwal1972,Haenggi1982a,Marconi2008,Baiesi2009,Seifert2010a} is that the response function can be formally expressed as a steady-state correlation function
L000262	\begin{align}
L000263	R_{k q}(t) = \Av{r_k(t) \psi_q(0)} .
L000264	\end{align}
L000265	The first crucial insight is that the quantity $\psi_q(t)$ has the same form as the observables \eqref{observable-current}; specifically,
L000266	\begin{align}
L000267	\psi_q(t) &= \tilde{z}_q(\bm{x}(t)) + \tilde{\bm{w}}_q(\bm{x}(t)) \circ \dot{\bm{x}}(t) \\
L000268	\tilde{z}_q(\bm{x}) &= - \frac{1}{2 \gamma^2} \bm{g}_q(\bm{x}) \cdot \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{f}(\bm{x}) - \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \grad_x \cdot \bm{g}_q(\bm{x}) \nn
L000269	\tilde{\bm{w}}_q(\bm{x}) &= -\frac{1}{2 \gamma^2} \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{g}_q(\bm{x}) \n .
L000270	\end{align}
L000271	The second crucial insight is that the correlation function of the \enquote{response observables} $\psi_q(t)$ is given by
L000272	\begin{align}
L000273	\Av{\psi_q(t) \psi_r(t')} = \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \Av{\bm{g}_q \cdot \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{g}_r}_\text{st} \delta(t-t') ,
L000274	\end{align}
L000275	which can be obtained by direct calculation, see Appendix A.
L000276	This result allows writing the joint spectral density matrix $\mathbb{S}(\omega)$ of the $K+Q$ observables $(r_1(t),\ldots,r_k(t),\psi_1(t),\ldots,\psi_Q(t))$ in block-wise form
L000277	\begin{align}
L000278	\mathbb{S}(\omega) = \begin{pmatrix} \bmc{S}(\omega) & \bmc{R}(\omega) \\ \bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) & \bmc{A} \end{pmatrix},
L000279	\end{align}
L000280	where the symmetric, positive definite matrix $\bmc{A}$ is defined as
L000281	\begin{align}
L000282	\mathcal{A}_{q r} = \int_{-\infty}^\infty dt \ e^{i \omega t} \Av{\psi_q(t) \psi_r(0)} = \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \Av{\bm{g}_q \cdot \bm{T}^{-1} \bm{g}_r}_\text{st} .
L000283	\end{align}
L000284	Since $\mathbb{S}(\omega)$ is a positive definite matrix, its blocks have to satisfy the conditions
L000285	\begin{subequations}
L000286	\begin{gather}
L000287	\bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) \bmc{S}^{-1}(\omega) \bmc{R}(\omega) \leq \bmc{A}, \label{response-tradeoff-1} \\
L000288	\bmc{R}(\omega) \bmc{A}^{-1} \bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) \leq \bmc{S}(\omega) \label{response-tradeoff-2} .
L000289	\end{gather} \label{response-tradeoff}
L000290	\end{subequations}
L000291	The first condition is precisely the first main result, \eqref{fffri}.
L000292	The second condition provides an alternative trade-off relation between the fluctuations and the response.
L000293	Integrating the second condition with respect to frequency yields, for configuration-dependent observables with $\bm{w}_q = 0$,
L000294	\begin{align}
L000295	\frac{1}{\pi} \int_{0}^\infty d\omega \ \bmc{R}(\omega) \bmc{A}^{-1} \bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) \leq \bm{\Xi}_\text{st}.
L000296	\end{align}
L000297	That is, the frequency-integral of the squared response matrix is bounded by the steady-state covariance matrix of the observables.
L000298	This generalizes \eqref{snr-bound} to the case of several observables and perturbations.
L000299	
L000300	\textit{Linear dynamics.}
L000301	Next, the above results are applied to a system of particles with linear interactions and perturbations that are constant in space.
L000302	In this case, \eqref{langevin-perturbed} is written as
L000303	\begin{align}
L000304	\dot{\bm{x}}(t) = \frac{1}{\gamma} \Big(-\bm{K} \big(\bm{x}(t) - \bm{x}_\text{eq} \big) + \epsilon \bmc{G} \bm{\phi}(t) \Big)  + \sqrt{\frac{2 \bm{T}}{\gamma}} \bm{\xi}(t) \label{langevin-linear} .
L000305	\end{align}
L000306	The matrix $\bmc{G} \in \mathbb{R}^{D \times Q}$ has the constant force $\bm{g}_q$ as its $q$-th column and $\bm{\phi}(t) = (\phi_1(t),\ldots,\phi_Q(t))$.
L000307	$\bm{K}$ is a matrix of spring constants, whose eigenvalues have positive real parts, so that the system has a stable steady state.
L000308	If $\bm{K}$ is symmetric, the resulting steady state is in equilibrium; if $\bm{K}$ is not symmetric, then non-reciprocal interactions drive the system into a non-equilibrium steady state.
L000309	Since both the dynamics and the perturbation in \eqref{langevin-linear} are linear, the linear response matrix \eqref{response} fully describes the response of the system for arbitrary magnitude $\epsilon$ of the perturbation.
L000310	The observables are likewise linear combinations of the coordinates of the particles, i.~e.~$\bm{z}(t) = \bmc{Z} \bm{x}(t)$ with $\bmc{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^{K \times D}$.
L000311	Since the system is linear, explicit expressions for the spectral density and response matrices are obtained (see Appendix B),
L000312	\begin{align}
L000313	\bmc{S}(\omega) &= \gamma \bmc{Z} \Big( \big( \bm{K} - i \omega \gamma \bm{I} \big)^{-1} \bm{\Xi}_{\text{st}} + [\text{h.c.}] \Big) \bmc{Z}^\text{T}, \nn
L000314	\bmc{R}(\omega) &= \bmc{Z} \big( \bm{K} - i\omega \gamma \bm{I} \big)^{-1} \bmc{G} , \\
L000315	\bmc{A} &= \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \bmc{G}^\text{T} \bm{T}^{-1} \bmc{G} \n .
L000316	\end{align}
L000317	Here, [h.c.] represents the Hermitian conjugate of the first matrix and $\bm{\Xi}_\text{st}$ denotes the steady-state covariance matrix of the coordinates.
L000318	With these expressions, it is a matter of straightforward linear algebra (see Appendix B) to verify the relations
L000319	\begin{subequations}
L000320	\begin{align}
L000321	\bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) \bmc{S}^{-1}(\omega) \bmc{R}(\omega) = \bmc{A} \quad &\text{if} \; \text{rank}(\bmc{Z}) = D \label{linear-identity-1} \\
L000322	\bmc{R}(\omega) \bmc{A}^{-1} \bmc{R}^\text{H}(\omega) = \bmc{S}(\omega) \quad &\text{if} \; \text{rank}(\bmc{G}) = D \label{linear-identity-2}.
L000323	\end{align}\label{linear-identity}
L000324	\end{subequations}
L000325	If the observables provide the complete information about the coordinates of the particles ($\bmc{Z}$ is an invertible matrix), then \eqref{response-tradeoff-1} is an equality.
L000326	Conversely, if the perturbations can reproduce any constant-in-space force ($\bmc{G}$ is an invertible matrix), then \eqref{response-tradeoff-2} is an equality.
L000327	Equality holds for both equilibrium and out-of-equilibrium situations, as long as the dynamics is linear.
L000328	
L000329	
L000330	\textit{Discussion.}
L000331	In this work, we derived a universal trade-off relation between the frequency response and the frequency-resolved fluctuations of discrete and continuous Markovian dynamics.
L000332	This fluctuation-response inequality quantifies the intuition that response and fluctuations are related even out of equilibrium, since they are both properties of the same underlying physical system.
L000333	We stress that \eqref{fffri} does not reduce to an equality in equilibrium and is thus distinct from the fluctuation-dissipation theorem.
L000334	Instead, we found that equality is attained for linear dynamics, independent of whether they are in or out of equilibrium.
L000335	Whether and how \eqref{fffri} can be related to the fluctuation-dissipation theorem and to the computation of dissipation from its violation \cite{Harada2005,Harada2006} is an open question.
L000336	
L000337	The derivation of \eqref{fffri} only requires the response to be represented as a correlation function of the unperturbed system.
L000338	Since this relation holds for general Markovian dynamics and possibly beyond, we expect that the result can be readily generalized to other dynamics, such as open quantum systems, where the corresponding zero-frequency result was recently obtained in Ref.~\cite{VanVu2025}.
L000339	A more challenging generalization is to derive similar bounds on non-linear response.
L000340	Recently, a relation between static non-linear response and first passage times was derived in Ref.~\cite{Bao2025}, but whether a similar result also applies to time-periodic perturbations is unclear.
L000341	Another approach is to characterize the system in the presence of a finite-strength periodic perturbation using Floquet theory and then apply the non-linear FRI derived in Ref.~\cite{Dechant2020}.
L000342	
L000343	The finite-frequency FRI \eqref{fffri} does not distinguish between systems that are in or out of equilibrium.
L000344	However, the relation between response and fluctuations is clearly different out of equilibrium, where the fluctuation-dissipation theorem does not hold.
L000345	Therefore, an important question is whether there are bounds on the response that explicitly take into account the non-equilibrium nature of the system.
L000346	One such relation was derived for the static response of currents in Ref.~\cite{Ptaszynski2024}, which is bounded by the entropy production rate.
L000347	Similar relations for the finite-frequency response might be obtained starting from the variational formula for the power-spectral density derived in Refs.~\cite{Dechant2023a,Vo2025}, which provides a formal identity between response the power spectrum, from which bounds can be derived.
